The issue you're encountering is due to how `deparse(substitute())` behaves when the argument is already evaluated before being passed to the function.

When you use `dataset = simple_addition`, the `simple_addition` variable is first evaluated to its value (the tibble), and then that value is passed to `task_create()`. By the time `substitute(dataset)` runs inside the function, it's capturing the already-evaluated tibble object, not the original variable name.

Here are a few ways to fix this:

## Option 1: Use NSE (Non-Standard Evaluation) approach

```r
task_create <- function(
    dataset,
    name = NULL,
    dir = eval_log_dir()
) {
  # Capture the unevaluated expression
  dataset_expr <- substitute(dataset)

  # If name is not provided, use the expression as name
  if (is.null(name)) {
    if (is.symbol(dataset_expr)) {
      name <- as.character(dataset_expr)
    } else {
      name <- "unnamed_dataset"
    }
  }

  # Rest of your function...
}
```

## Option 2: Force users to provide a name or dataset as a symbol

```r
task_create <- function(
    dataset,
    name = NULL,
    dir = eval_log_dir()
) {
  # Get the call that invoked this function
  call <- match.call()

  # If name is NULL, try to get it from the call
  if (is.null(name) && !is.null(call$dataset) && is.symbol(call$dataset)) {
    name <- as.character(call$dataset)
  } else if (is.null(name)) {
    name <- "unnamed_dataset"
  }

  # Rest of your function...
}
```

## Option 3: Use enquo/quo_name from rlang (tidyverse approach)

```r
library(rlang)

task_create <- function(
    dataset,
    name = NULL,
    dir = eval_log_dir()
) {
  # Capture the expression using rlang
  dataset_quo <- enquo(dataset)

  # Use the expression as name if name is not provided
  if (is.null(name) && quo_is_symbol(dataset_quo)) {
    name <- quo_name(dataset_quo)
  } else if (is.null(name)) {
    name <- "unnamed_dataset"
  }

  # Rest of your function...
}
```

Any of these approaches should give you the variable name "simple_addition" rather than the evaluated tibble contents in your example.

